<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Brunswick-Lüneburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1235 CE – 1269 CE · capital Brunswick</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Duchy created in 1235 for Otto the Child, grandson of Henry the Lion, which reconciled the Guelphs with the Hohenstaufen and founded the house of Brunswick.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion's</a> fall in 1180, his family kept their allodial lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>. At the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> in 1235 his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-the-child">Otto the Child</a> surrendered these lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Emperor Frederick II</a> and received them back as a new imperial duchy, ending the long feud between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Otto ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion's</a> castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dankwarderode-castle">Dankwarderode</a> and the bronze lion stood, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, whose salt works were the duchy's main source of wealth. The duchy lay between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celle">Celle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>. After Otto's death in 1252 his sons Albert I the Tall and John ruled jointly.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1269 Albert and John divided the duchy, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Principality of Wolfenbüttel</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-luneburg">Principality of Lüneburg</a>. The ducal title stayed common to all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelph</a> lines, from which the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hanover">Electorate of Hanover</a> and the British royal house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> later descended.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-brunswick-luneburg.jpg" alt="Duchy of Brunswick-Lüneburg" width="320" height="242"><figcaption>Image: Interchange88 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brunswick-L%C3%BCneburg_Locator_1648.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1235 CE – 1269 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Brunswick</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Otto the Child, Albert I the Tall, John of Lüneburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Braunschweig-Lüneburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1252&amp;focus=duchy-of-brunswick-luneburg" aria-label="Duchy of Brunswick-Lüneburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4917 373 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Brunswick-Lüneburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5237 629L5229 694M5237 629L5232 601M5237 629L5237 632"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5232 601h0M5237 632h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5237 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5237 629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Brunswick. <a href="/atlas?year=1252&amp;focus=duchy-of-brunswick-luneburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
